(396100, 32391, 656, '<td valign="top">Critique Club Comment:<br/> <br/>Wow - very high initial impact when I opened the pictures.  great colours, lots of textures and an interesting pattern that leads me around the shot.<br/> <br/>The processing you''ve done makes it look very computer generated/ solarised but the underlying picture is surreal anyway so suits the treatment I think.  <br/> <br/>As a photograph it feels generally a bit softer than it might be but is a very dramatic effect.  Not surprised that it tanked here at dpc but that doesn''t mean its a bad image.<br/> <br/></td>', 'Critique Club Comment: Wow - very high initial impact when I opened the pictures.  great colours, lots of textures and an interesting pattern that leads me around the shot. The processing you''ve done makes it look very computer generated/ solarised but the underlying picture is surreal anyway so suits the treatment I think.   As a photograph it feels generally a bit softer than it might be but is a very dramatic effect.  Not surprised that it tanked here at dpc but that doesn''t mean its a bad image. ', '2003-08-22 13:07:26.000000', NULL, 0),
(396114, 32829, 656, '<td valign="top">CCC: You are right in your comments that it is a bit cutsie, but I think the soft focus is subtly done and works quite well to give an ''etheral glow'' to the people.<br/> <br/>Compositionally, the shot seems a bit too balanced between the brown at the bottom and the green regions at the top - I think maybe less water would work well.<br/> <br/>The way you''ve framed the people works well to focus on them.</td>', 'CCC: You are right in your comments that it is a bit cutsie, but I think the soft focus is subtly done and works quite well to give an ''etheral glow'' to the people. Compositionally, the shot seems a bit too balanced between the brown at the bottom and the green regions at the top - I think maybe less water would work well. The way you''ve framed the people works well to focus on them.', '2003-08-22 13:17:06.000000', NULL, 0),
(396592, 32311, 11339, '<td valign="top">this photo is too good,<br/>I love the idea, how did you do this?<br/>good job</td>', 'this photo is too good,I love the idea, how did you do this?good job', '2003-08-22 19:07:24.000000', NULL, 0),
(396787, 32873, 6100, '<td valign="top">I like the light at the time of day at which you made this picture. The evening light, being low, helps to provide modeling to the buildings making them stand out. I would like to see the horizon appear straighter and either lower or higher in the composition rather than so close to the center. (As an aside, there seems to be a white spot in the sky which does not appear to be a cloud --- upper right side. I know ... no spot editing ... but you might want to blow out the interior of your D100. I do it almost every time I change lenses.) The evening light also gave you a lower contrast range overall and helped you capture detail in most of the shadowed areas along with the brighter areas. That''s always nice to see. <br/> <br/>Without the title, there is no indication of the appropriateness to the challenge although one could surmise that you must have been inside SOMETHING and you were obviously looking out. Although I don''t think it affected how I scored your picture, it just isn''t as obvious as some of the others in the challenge. <br/> <br/>Regards ... from the Critique Club,<br/> <br/>Bob Mahan<br/>(rmahan)<br/></td>', 'I like the light at the time of day at which you made this picture. The evening light, being low, helps to provide modeling to the buildings making them stand out. I would like to see the horizon appear straighter and either lower or higher in the composition rather than so close to the center. (As an aside, there seems to be a white spot in the sky which does not appear to be a cloud --- upper right side. I know ... no spot editing ... but you might want to blow out the interior of your D100. I do it almost every time I change lenses.) The evening light also gave you a lower contrast range overall and helped you capture detail in most of the shadowed areas along with the brighter areas. That''s always nice to see.  Without the title, there is no indication of the appropriateness to the challenge although one could surmise that you must have been inside SOMETHING and you were obviously looking out. Although I don''t think it affected how I scored your picture, it just isn''t as obvious as some of the others in the challenge.  Regards ... from the Critique Club, Bob Mahan(rmahan)', '2003-08-22 23:06:36.000000', NULL, 0),
(397094, 32277, 5112, '<td valign="top">FROM THE CRITIQUE CLUB<br/> <br/>Hi, Kathy,<br/> <br/>I was out of town during the "Inside Looking Out" voting, so this is the first time I see this photo.  I like it.<br/> <br/>Composition:  I think your composition is very good, the close crop is excellent.  At the same time, for this particular challenge the close crop probably worked against you, because without the context it is a bit hard to see what the little frog is in, what he''s looking out of.  I like the bowl like feeling at the bottom of the picture.  It''s interesting that the frog is centered, yet the picture uses the rule of thirds quite well.<br/> <br/>Focus:  Pretty good.  It looks a tad on the soft side, low light probably had something to do with that?<br/> <br/>Colour/Light:  You used flash, right?  The greens are beautiful, but on the frog they look like they got a bit too much light and thus lost detail.  I think it''s interesting how the whole picture is tones of black to white, greens and a little bit of brownish/yellow.  The flash (light) spots on the frog''s nose, eyebumps, and right above the head take away a bit from the beauty of the picture.  I think that given the circumstances in which I imagine you took this picture, this is actually quite good - it would be great if you could do the same picture under different lighting circumstances.<br/> <br/>Overall - a good entry to the challenge, a great pose - good work!<br/> <br/>Take care,<br/> <br/>Ursula (uabresch)<br/>Comments, complaints, questions ... feel free to contact me</td>', 'FROM THE CRITIQUE CLUB Hi, Kathy, I was out of town during the "Inside Looking Out" voting, so this is the first time I see this photo.  I like it. Composition:  I think your composition is very good, the close crop is excellent.  At the same time, for this particular challenge the close crop probably worked against you, because without the context it is a bit hard to see what the little frog is in, what he''s looking out of.  I like the bowl like feeling at the bottom of the picture.  It''s interesting that the frog is centered, yet the picture uses the rule of thirds quite well. Focus:  Pretty good.  It looks a tad on the soft side, low light probably had something to do with that? Colour/Light:  You used flash, right?  The greens are beautiful, but on the frog they look like they got a bit too much light and thus lost detail.  I think it''s interesting how the whole picture is tones of black to white, greens and a little bit of brownish/yellow.  The flash (light) spots on the frog''s nose, eyebumps, and right above the head take away a bit from the beauty of the picture.  I think that given the circumstances in which I imagine you took this picture, this is actually quite good - it would be great if you could do the same picture under different lighting circumstances. Overall - a good entry to the challenge, a great pose - good work! Take care, Ursula (uabresch)Comments, complaints, questions ... feel free to contact me', '2003-08-23 11:15:47.000000', NULL, 0),
(397182, 32775, 5112, '<td valign="top">FROM THE CRITIQUE CLUB<br/> <br/>Hi, Jason,<br/> <br/>I''m a bit worried about doing a critique on this photo, as you seem to have been quite frustrated with the voting results.  And I think that you are right, the score is low for the quality of the picture.  BTW - I wasn''t around that week, so I didn''t vote :)))<br/> <br/>I think that your commenters that didn''t see how the picture fit in the challenge might have a bit of a point, in particular if you think the fit has to be "in your face", but I think that you knew that would happen when you entered the picture.  I also think though that it would have been easy to imagine a caged bird trying to escape, and that it''s worth remembering that pictures don''t have to be "in your face" to meet a challenge, that oftentimes pictures that make you think, dream, imagine, are the ones you''ll remember.  So, I guess I''m being slightly critical of the lack of imagination of the voters.<br/> <br/>As far as the picture itself:<br/> <br/>Composition:  IMO the composition is great.  I like the bigger top (or, the crop you chose).  I like the grey/white background, I think it works really well here, as do the shadows.  The "pose" of the bird is fantastic!  To me, besides plotting to escape, he looks like he''s trying to hide his thoughts from the viewer.  The simple black frame is very good.<br/> <br/>Focus/Sharpness:  Looks fine to me.  I love the slightly OOF feathers to the front.<br/> <br/>Light/Colour:  Overall quite good, in particular in the front and background.  The subtle brown of the feathers is beautiful, as are the greys in the shadows.  The black/white/reds of the main focal plane are also beautiful, but they look a tad like they got a bit too much light (did you use flash for this picture?) and lost a bit of detail.  I wish the eye were "shinier" or a bit more detailed.<br/> <br/>Overall:  IMO this is a good picture, interesting to look at, it tells a story, makes you imagine a situation.  Taking into account the nature of the voters on DPC, it probably wasn''t the best choice for the challenge.  But don''t worry, scores are just scores, but you do excellent, imaginative work!<br/> <br/>Take care,<br/> <br/>Ursula (uabresch)<br/>Complaints, questions ... email me.<br/> <br/>EDIT (Addition)<br/> <br/>2 things I forgot to say, <br/>(1) the title is great!<br/>(2) this picture looks a lot better big than as a thumbnail.<br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2003-08-23 12:44:49.</i></td>', 'FROM THE CRITIQUE CLUB Hi, Jason, I''m a bit worried about doing a critique on this photo, as you seem to have been quite frustrated with the voting results.  And I think that you are right, the score is low for the quality of the picture.  BTW - I wasn''t around that week, so I didn''t vote :))) I think that your commenters that didn''t see how the picture fit in the challenge might have a bit of a point, in particular if you think the fit has to be "in your face", but I think that you knew that would happen when you entered the picture.  I also think though that it would have been easy to imagine a caged bird trying to escape, and that it''s worth remembering that pictures don''t have to be "in your face" to meet a challenge, that oftentimes pictures that make you think, dream, imagine, are the ones you''ll remember.  So, I guess I''m being slightly critical of the lack of imagination of the voters. As far as the picture itself: Composition:  IMO the composition is great.  I like the bigger top (or, the crop you chose).  I like the grey/white background, I think it works really well here, as do the shadows.  The "pose" of the bird is fantastic!  To me, besides plotting to escape, he looks like he''s trying to hide his thoughts from the viewer.  The simple black frame is very good. Focus/Sharpness:  Looks fine to me.  I love the slightly OOF feathers to the front. Light/Colour:  Overall quite good, in particular in the front and background.  The subtle brown of the feathers is beautiful, as are the greys in the shadows.  The black/white/reds of the main focal plane are also beautiful, but they look a tad like they got a bit too much light (did you use flash for this picture?) and lost a bit of detail.  I wish the eye were "shinier" or a bit more detailed. Overall:  IMO this is a good picture, interesting to look at, it tells a story, makes you imagine a situation.  Taking into account the nature of the voters on DPC, it probably wasn''t the best choice for the challenge.  But don''t worry, scores are just scores, but you do excellent, imaginative work! Take care, Ursula (uabresch)Complaints, questions ... email me. EDIT (Addition) 2 things I forgot to say, (1) the title is great!(2) this picture looks a lot better big than as a thumbnail.', '2003-08-23 13:42:27.000000', '2003-08-23 13:44:49.000000', 0),
